{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. Summary:\n\n**Why the second publish 500'd:** `similarity()` (`src/app.py:30`) returns a Python float, which lands in `item[\"similar\"][i][\"score\"]`. boto3's DynamoDB serializer rejects floats (`TypeError: Float types are not supported` — the exact error in `logs/error.log`). The first publish only succeeded because the table was empty: `related()` returned `[]`, no score was computed, no float reached the write. Once any story existed, every publish crashed.\n\n**Fix:** `similarity()` now returns `Decimal(str(round(..., 4)))` — DynamoDB-safe, and the `str()` conversion keeps the exact rounded value (`Decimal('0.4029')`) instead of binary-float noise. The 0–1 score format is unchanged.\n\n**So it can't come back:** the old test only ever published into an empty table — the very gap that let this ship. Added `test_publish_second_story_with_existing_record` (`tests/test_publish.py:29`), which seeds a record, publishes a second, and asserts the stored score is a `Decimal`. The fake table validates types like boto3, so any future float fails the test exactly as a real table would.\n\n**Verified:** I reproduced the pre-fix failure (second publish raised the log's exact `TypeError`), then after the fix ran the full suite (2 tests pass) and published three stories through `handler()` — all 201.\n\n**From the colleague's note:** applied — the root-cause framing (\"the bug doesn't exist until the data does\"), the seeded-table testing lesson, and publish-twice verification. Adapted — the note's fix (int percentages) was replaced with `Decimal` to preserve the existing score format; that's all recorded in `ANSWER.md`."}
